<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alessandro Volta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Alessandro Giuseppe Antonio Anastasio Volta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alessandro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> was an Italian chemist and physicist from Como, credited as the inventor of the electric battery and the discoverer of methane, which he found in the marshes of Angera on Lake Maggiore in 1776. He invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">voltaic pile</a> in 1799 and reported it in a letter to the president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, published in 1800, showing that electricity could be generated chemically; the invention led to the field of electrochemistry. He held the chair of experimental physics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a> for almost 40 years and was honoured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. The SI unit of electric potential, the volt, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alessandro-volta.jpg" alt="Alessandro Volta" width="330" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alessandro_Volta.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
